STEP 2 — EXISTING CONTENT CHECK
Before analyzing the query data, ask if I've attached my existing published 
content list (URLs/titles). If I have, use it as the deduplication filter for 
every recommendation you make in this session — never suggest a topic that 
substantially overlaps with something already live. If a query cluster is 
close to existing content but the existing page has low CTR or isn't 
ranking well for it, flag that as a REFRESH opportunity instead of a NEW 
PAGE opportunity, and say why (e.g. thin content, wrong intent match, 
outdated title).

STEP 3 — AUDIT EACH FILE IN THE GROUP
For each uploaded Excel file, do the following:
- Sort by impressions descending, look at the top 50-100 rows (or all rows 
  if fewer)
- Cluster queries into topic groups based on shared intent/subject 
  (e.g. "almond nail shapes", "gel removal how-to", "prom nail ideas")
- For each cluster, report: total combined impressions, total clicks, 
  average CTR, and 3-5 representative sample queries
- Flag clusters with high impressions but low CTR — these are visibility 
  without conversion, usually a title/meta/content-intent mismatch on an 
  existing page, or a missing page entirely
- Cross-check every cluster against my existing content list before calling 
  it a "new page" opportunity

STEP 4 — OUTPUT FORMAT
Give me a table with these columns:
| Cluster/Topic | Status (New Page / Refresh Existing / Already Covered Well) | Combined Impressions | Avg CTR | Sample Queries | Suggested Content Type (guide, collection page, FAQ, comparison, etc.) | Priority (High/Med/Low) |

Priority = High if impressions are large AND (no existing page OR existing 
page has low CTR). Priority = Low if impressions are small or intent is 
ambiguous.

After the table, give me a short written summary (5-8 bullets) naming the 
single best content opportunity from this batch and why.

STEP 5 — WAIT
After delivering Step 4's output for one group, stop and ask if I want to 
move to the next group, or dig deeper into any specific cluster from this one 
(e.g. draft an outline, check search intent further, compare against a 
competitor).

RULES
- Never suggest a topic that duplicates existing content unless it's 
  explicitly a "Refresh" recommendation
- Always ground recommendations in the actual query data I upload — no 
  generic nail-industry suggestions not backed by GSC evidence
- If a regex file has fewer than 20 rows or impressions are negligible 
  (under ~50 total), say so plainly instead of forcing a content 
  recommendation
- Keep cluster names short and usable as working titles, not vague labels
- For Group 2's regex #19 and Group 5's regex #23, note that they still 
  contain unfilled placeholders ([your city], [your state], [city1], 
  [city2]) — if I haven't swapped these in GSC before exporting, the file 
  may be empty or invalid; flag this rather than guessing at results
